shouldn't rage make you faster ranger  smart weapon  hey guys Joey here and I hope you've all  had a wonderful release week with  injustice too because it's a damn fine  game but hey don't take my word for it  head on over to GameSpot calm and check  out Peter Brown's full review of the  game now if you're anything like me and  your background is mostly in Capcom  fighting games you might find injustice  too just a little bit weird the combo  system is way different from Street  Fighter and there are so many little  mechanics that you might miss when you  first get your hands on the game so what  I've done is I've come up with 6 things  that have helped meet and will hopefully  help you get started in defending or  destroying which everyone you choose the  DC Universe let's get started  so like any fighting game combos are  among the most important things to learn  and injustice to utilizes basic preset  combos that are easily executed by  pressing any combination of light medium  and heavy attack but unlike Street  Fighter these combos don't require  specific timing they're more like target  combos whereas long as the buns are hit  quickly enough they'll still come out  which means the main thing you're gonna  want to practice is linking special  moves afterwards  now not all basic combos will allow you  to do that but once you find the right  ones you'll be doing some heavy damage  in no time there's also one more big  difference between injustice and other  fighting games when using jumping  attacks there's actually no need to wait  until you land before pressing the next  button so for example once the jumping  attack hits you can start inputting the  rest of the combo even before you hit  the ground again timing is crucial to  get this one right but hey practice  makes perfect  one of the more unique things about  injustice games are the environmental  objects you can wall up your opponent  with any time you're close to an object  that you can interact with you'll see it  glow and the small icon will pop up near  your health bar indicating that it's  time to smash an alligator over their  head now different characters will  interact with objects in different  weights bigger buffer characters will  usually grab the object and throw it at  your opponent while smaller more agile  characters will leap off of it kind of  like an extra wall Djem while it is  pretty satisfying to just grab the  object and whack them with it with the  right position you can actually  incorporate the environments into your  combos themselves and of course don't  forget about stage transitions hitting  your opponent with back-heavy near the  edge of specific stages will trigger the  transition resulting in a nice cutscene  and a good chunk of damage and yes you  can cancel a combo into a wall bounce  for the same effect  with so many fighters on the roster  injustice to gives you a ton of  different fighting styles to choose from  now the question is which one's for you  my advice would be to ask yourself how  you like to play but if you're looking  for personal recommendations here are my  thoughts aggressive players who liked  even the pressure are in luck since many  of the characters are pretty capable of  doing that  Superman Batman lead the way here being  two of the most balanced and powerful  characters but Wonder Woman cheetah  Robin and the flash can all rush down  with the best of them if you enjoy  projectiles and controlling space  between your opponent's Brainiac Green  Arrow and dr. fate will pretty much do  just that if you like getting close and  tossing your opponent's around both Bane  and swampthing will satisfy your  grappler needs and for those of you who  just want to mess with your opponents  with high lows and cross ups I suggest  Harley Quinn or a Supergirl but if  you're feeling particularly nasty  Captain cold is the way to go the  icicles that he can shoot onto the  ceiling which then drop down presents  some great options for wake ups  if you find yourself getting stuck in  combos more often than you'd like the  clash mechanic is probably where you'll  find the most help when you're on your  second health bar holding forward and  the meter burn button while gain hit  will prompt a small cutscene between you  and your opponent and you'll be able to  wager your meter to stop their combo and  heal yourself a little now this is where  true mind games come in because if you  have more meter you could bet at all to  make sure you win or you could try to  bet less and save your meter with the  idea being that your opponent will know  they'll lose anyway and not bet anything  but then they might know that and then  bet more yeah there's a lot of circular  reasoning going on here but you get the  idea basically more meter equals good  no you can leave the bowling pins at  home for this one I'm talking about  juggling with combos here arguably the  most crucial part of in Justices combo  system is the juggle which happens when  you hit your opponents in the air and  have the opportunity to continue the  combo some moves like back and forth  heavy will cause huge bounces giving you  a better chance of juggling but each  character has a bunch of moves that will  juggle as well basically it's all about  linking together the right chain to keep  them in the air it'll definitely take  some time and practice but there's  nothing more satisfying than landing  that perfect combo to take off half  their life using meter burns with their  special moves is a great way to extend  combos and pile on some extra damage but  it can take some time to get used to a  lot of the moves in the game require you  to press the meter burn button right  after a move connects so it can be hard  to do on reaction but that's not the  only way you can use a meter per if  you're in the middle of a combo string  pressing down forward or down back and  meter bird will cancer a combo into a  ground or wall bounce giving you that  chance to get in way more damage but  meter burn is also a great defensive  tool as well with enough meter you can  use it to push block execute a full  screen roll or even pop yourself out of  the middle of a combo the game requires  a bunch of resource management so choose  your meter burns wisely unless you be  caught in a deadly combo with no way out  [Applause]  so those are six things that really  helped me and learning the combo system  and mechanics and justice but if you  have any other tips for people just  starting out leave them in the comments  because I would love to hear some more  strategies combo ideas and anything that  could help me and everyone else just get  better at the game so if you want to see  more injustice videos like this one make  sure to hit that subscribe button and  I'll see you guys next time  [Music]
 

